        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130276",
        ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"
